{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: the R14 usage-report parser fix for the provider's limit hint (child 686f4ec9). Head c10c75980ffa98a1a8acce7b27a6031fbc0a8113, branch request/security-r14-limit-hint-20261027. Main 6499a12b is the merge base. The delta is 5 paths, 451 insertions and 9 deletions. Review request 1337d81c, checker promise b1af04ad, request 686f4ec9, builder promise 17847f26, after the planner's first run 98833a92. Primary artifact 807849e6 (the PD-2 note), first of 8. gs inspect shows it resting on promise 17847f26 and request 686f4ec9.\n\nRead order: the note in full; 686f4ec9 and 98833a92; the script, test and docs diffs; the raw control output and restoration record; the gate record; the manifest and the sealed directory. I did not run any suite again.\n\nRequest conditions (686f4ec9):\n1. Truncation by the query's own limit. The one-line check that threw query-limit-hint is removed, and rows_before_limit_at_least is now ignored. The note and the comment say why: the figure counts source rows before GROUP BY, not returned groups. Truncation is still judged the way it was: the SQL asks for 8,193 groups, and more than 8,192 refuses the response. rows must still equal the data length, and every other envelope, type, numeric, mapping, credential and transport check is unchanged.\n2. Recorded-shape witness. A new case rebuilds the envelope the planner observed, with every value scripted TEST data and no provider rows: keys data, meta, rows and rows_before_limit_at_least; String labels; UInt64 counts as decimal strings; UInt32 counters and times; Float64 aggregates; rows as a number; 67 groups; hint 350,918. It decodes all 67 groups with exact numbers. The paired assertion in the same case refuses 8,193 groups under the same hint. The old provider-hint case is removed from the truncation test, and its cap-plus-one and row-count cases remain.\n3. Control (raw output read). Restoring the old hint check makes the new case fail at its named assertion (\"input-row hint must not reject 67 returned groups\"; pass 0, fail 1). The restoration record shows the exact bytes restored.\n4. docs/usage-report.md says what the figure means, that the parser ignores it, and that end-to-end compatibility still waits for the planner's rerun.\n\nValidation:\n- The ordinary gate at 9bbf4a9e (tree b24a320f) passed: clean before and after, no signal, Node 66, Counting 55, plain 2,863 passed and 1 skipped, Native 796 passed and 2 skipped, 0 unhandled, 0 contradictory records. The note gives 1,243.6 s wall and 2,536.2 s child CPU (one run).\n- After the gate only the note and the manifest change, so the documents-only rule applies.\n- The manifest verifies against the sealed directory: 66 files, 0 bad, nothing writable, SHA256SUMS all OK.\n- check-notes 6499a12b..c10c7598 reports 0 warnings, and git diff --check is clean.\n- check-doc-links on an exported tree of the head: 712 relative links checked, 0 broken.\n\nArchitecture: sound. A one-line removal. The completeness rule now rests only on what the script itself asked for.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The token, mapping and output boundaries are untouched. Ignoring the figure cannot turn a truncated response into a complete one, because the group cap is checked first and independently.\n\nSimplification: acceptable, with one nit. The envelope list still allows rows_before_limit_at_least, but its value is no longer validated, so any value is accepted. That is harmless while the value is ignored. If it is ever reported, validate it there.\n\nVerdict: approved at exact head c10c7598. The builder lands. The landing closes child 686f4ec9 only; parent R14 bb763efa and 0f0eeab7 stay open. No builder deployment. The planner reruns the script and files the first complete report under bb763efa.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:807849e66c57f85caac45ac48d048470652c6589","binding":"assigned","head":"c10c75980ffa98a1a8acce7b27a6031fbc0a8113","head_news_acknowl